{"id":"revenue-port-rent-share","title":"Port tenants that pay rent on their sales","question":"Among Port tenants whose rent depends on their sales, does any pay a far smaller share of sales than its peers, and can the pandemic years be checked?","finding":"Nothing unusual in the last report on disk. The Port’s report for 2020-02 lists 70 tenant lines that paid $34.1 million in rent on $282.6 million of sales over the twelve months before. Full-service restaurants paid a middle 7.0% of sales (lowest 5.1%, highest 24%). 1 line paid half or less of the middle share for the category, and 10 paid only the minimum rent with nothing on top. The reports on disk run from 2015-11 to 2020-02 (50 months); none was found after that, so rent forgiven or deferred from March 2020 on cannot be tested from the published records.","how":"The Port’s monthly sales and rent report for 2020-02, the latest on disk: every tenant line with a rent figure for the last twelve months. Rent share is total rent divided by reported sales; the peer figure is the middle share among lines of the same category in the same report; “against peers” divides one by the other. Lines are sorted lowest first. A tenant line that reads as a private person’s name is shown without the name.","limits":"The report covers only tenants on percentage rent (restaurants, shops, parking operators, excursion boats), not the Port’s whole rent roll, and its categories are broad: a museum, an aquarium and a shipyard sit together under “other”, so a low share there says little. Each lease sets its own percentage and minimum, which the report does not print; a tenant paying only the minimum did not sell enough to owe more. Sales are the tenants’ own figures and the report’s footnotes mark some as unavailable. What would settle the pandemic years: the Port’s rent roll and its record of rent forgiven or deferred, tenant by tenant, which are not published.","tier":"CLEARED","columns":[{"k":"tenant","t":"Tenant line","type":"org"